From ud1acffd991fa4452 at groups.google.com  Wed Jun 20 12:36:30 2012
From: ud1acffd991fa4452 at groups.google.com (rene.rotterdam)
Date: Wed, 20 Jun 2012 12:36:30 +0000
Subject: adjustment for background noise on human exon array
Message-ID: <weCIokJAC0s.CQmt1Z4T1zwJ@aroma-affymetrix.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/aroma-affymetrix/c/weCIokJAC0s/m/CQmt1Z4T1zwJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Dear all,

I am currently comparing two independent data sets (Human Exon Array) towards common features and in order to do so, I want to discard probesets that are lowly expressed to reduce noise.
However, it is not applicable to just set a threshold for both data sets, as the distribution of intensity values is shifted (though it's overall shape is the same).
To clarify: the median log2 intensity in set 1 is 4.55, whereas in the other one it is 6.45.
Therefore I wanted to adapt some sort of detection above background (as known from Affymetrix Power Tools) or a similar algorithm.

However, DABG utilizes the control probes present on the exon array to determine whether a probeset is present or not. Since I used the fullR3 CDF (http://www.aroma-project.org/node/122) for my analysis so far,
the list of probesets does not include any control probes. So is there any other way to determine the background noise? I read about a method called MAT (http://genomebiology.com/2007/8/5/R82/#B31), but the authors trained their model on either background or full probes (which I do want to use for analysis). I'm grateful for any ideas.

Best regards,
René

